00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:14.620
Earlier this month, China made history by
becoming the first country to land softly

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.650
on the far side of the moon.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:22.180
What makes this mission especially difficult
is that, unlike those on the near side, this

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.690
one never has a direct view of Earth.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:31.699
To communicate, the rover has to use a special
relay satellite, positioned between them.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:37.200
But for all that extra work, the Chang'e 4
gives China international recognition.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.280
It’s not just another landing among many.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.260
And this is just the beginning.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:50.800
In 2017, France spent two billion dollars
on space, Russia, three billion, the European

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.180
Space Agency, six, and China, eleven.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:00.920
And sure, that’s still far less than NASA,
at almost 20 billion,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:07.460
But the U.S. also spends three times more
on healthcare for the same life expectancy,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.970
so money isn’t everything.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:15.210
China is also opening up to private space
companies, which quietly compete with those

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.580
in America, like SpaceX and Blue Origin.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:25.780
Spacety, for example, specializes in micro-satellites
weighing as little as 3 pounds each, a previously

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.570
unfilled, but profitable, niche.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.950
This rapid increase of Chinese investment
in space will generate thousands of jobs and

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.799
countless scientific discoveries.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:39.440
But it isn’t just about space,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:46.250
It’s also a calculated, military strategy
with global consequences.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:55.250
30,000 feet in the air, moving 800 kilometers
per hour, you can watch this very video thanks

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:00.630
to the $260 billion a year industry that is…
satellites.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:06.780
With them, we can predict the weather, snoop
on our neighbors, and, of course, watch TV.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:13.170
For free, by the way, if you live in Dish,
Texas, which legally changed its name in 2005

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:16.010
in exchange for 200 free channels.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.420
Could be worse.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:23.830
But perhaps most important are the 31 satellites
which form the Global Positioning System.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.500
Cargo ships, carrying 90% of the world’s
goods, use it to navigate.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.010
Precision farming makes plowing, planting,
and fertilizing vastly more efficient.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:40.170
Pilots use it to fly, Surveyors to measure
boundaries, and you and I, to get delivered

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.490
the finest Italian cuisine money can buy.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:48.970
But determining where you are is, arguably,
not even its most important function.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.630
It’s also the world’s clock - keeping
the time for everything from the New York

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:59.560
Stock Exchange, to credit card transactions,
and ATMs, with an accuracy of one billionth

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.810
of a second.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:06.470
Its no exaggeration to call GPS one of the
foundations of the global economy.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.489
So, what does it cost?

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:10.879
Absolutely nothing.

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:14.480
If you’re an American taxpayer, it’s ”included”.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:21.569
But for everyone else, this $1.4 billion a
year service is provided 100% free.

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:28.950
There’s just one catch: GPS is owned and
operated by the United States Air Force.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:34.390
Like the internet, it was originally developed
by and only for, the military.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:43.390
But in 1983, Korean Air flight 007 from Anchorage
to Seoul made a very costly navigation mistake.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:48.980
Instead of flying around restricted Soviet
airspace, it unintentionally flew over the

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:54.580
Kamchatka peninsula, and on the same day as
a missile test at the same location.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:04:01.370
After tracking the aircraft for over an hour,
it was shot down, killing all 269 people on

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.420
board.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:08.360
To prevent this from happening again, President
Reagan made GPS freely available for civilian

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.360
use.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.080
And in 2000, President Clinton ended Selective
Availability, granting everyone, not just

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.079
the military, access the same level of accuracy.

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:24.310
Officially, quote “It is not the intent
of the U.S. to ever use Selective Availability

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.310
again.”,

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:30.950
Which, notably, is not the same as “We can’t
deny access ever again”.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:37.820
During the war between India and Pakistan
in 1999, the U.S. refused to give India access

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.480
to GPS data.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:45.980
Much more recently, in 2012, a disabled GPS
satellite was blamed for a failed Russia-India

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.550
missile test.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.240
That uncertainty is enough to make foreign
countries nervous.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.690
And now consider that this hugely important,
economy-running technology can be interrupted

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:00.660
with a $10 spoofing device.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.160
Because GPS signals are weak by the time they
reach the ground, it’s remarkably easy to

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.130
emit a stronger, competing signal.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:14.160
This device, for example, can trick nearby
cars into going the wrong way or crashing

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.490
into other vehicles.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:22.990
In 2008, Newark airport became the first in
America to use the FAA’s NextGen GPS system

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:27.840
for tracking planes, which frequently, inexplicably
stopped working.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.320
A three-year investigation found a trucker
on the nearby New Jersey Turnpike using a

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.090
GPS jammer to stop his employer from tracking
him.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:43.030
Like the drone which shut down the UK’s
second busiest airport in December, GPS jamming

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:48.080
is so dangerous because it costs so little
to do so much damage.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:55.229
One, single drone, 100,000 passengers and
760 flights affected.

00:05:55.229 --> 00:06:01.170
For all these reasons, several countries are
launching their own alternatives to GPS.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:06.470
Russia has GLONASS, The EU has Galileo, and
China, Beidou.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:10.130
Technically, none of these systems are directly
competing.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.290
60% of receivers use at least two of them
simultaneously.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:20.860
But it’s also in each country’s interest
to control as much navigation as possible.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:26.250
It’s also part of China’s goal to secure
its claim to the South China Sea.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:32.590
The problem is, just about everyone is watching,
including the U.S., which routinely sends

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:34.190
ships to patrol the region.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.880
In October, one of them nearly collided with
a Chinese destroyer.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:45.500
So, the challenge for China is asserting its
claim without risking conflict between 2 nuclear

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:46.500
powers.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.370
Its solution are nicknamed the “little blue
men” - local fishermen sponsored by the

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:56.069
government to work in the area and report
foreign activity to the military.

00:06:56.069 --> 00:07:02.740
This way, it asserts its claim, benefits economically,
and does so in a less-threatening way.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.740
But these fishermen have become a target for
nearby countries, like Indonesia.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.210
Which is where Beidou comes in.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:16.940
Unlike GPS, China’s version allows receivers
to send short messages and request military

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.000
assistance.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.990
It gives fishermen the full power of the People’s
Liberation Army without carrying a single

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:24.990
weapon.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.990
This is the tension created by China’s ambitions
in space.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:35.330
Whether it’s purely motivated by research
and science or not, doesn’t really matter.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:40.460
Either way, it’s going to be interpreted
aggressively by established powers like the

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.120
United States.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:46.660
Space technology is, at least for now, closely
tied to the military.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:52.940
It’s just one more way the world will have
to adapt to, or risk escalation with, the

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.630
meteoric rise of China.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:08:01.030
Investing in space is about exploring the
unknown for the sake of discovering new things.

